\editor controls the editor pane it is executed in.
It does nothing by itself and takes no arguments, you must
call one of its methods, concatenated with a dot.

To highlight the notes in the editor while they are played
by the sequencer, call:

\editor.animate

To turn this highlighting off, call:

\editor.inanimate

To align the melodic lines of bar definitions, so that the
notes that sound together are written under each other,
call:

\editor.alignment on

As a score engraver spaces notes, the shortest duration of
a bar is the unit of its grid, so a bar of quarters is
narrower than a bar of 16ths. One unit is as wide as the
longest note token of the bar (with its articulations and
dynamics, but not its text), and the notes of tuplets are
placed in proportion between the units. Text in quotes,
and \ottava, \ott, and \clef with their arguments, make
room only where they are, so a line that starts with \clef
starts the notes of all the lines after it. In \bars,
every bar is aligned separately.
To make a unit as wide as the shortest note token of the
bar instead, so that a longer token makes room only where
it is, as text does, call:

\editor.alignment short

and to go back to the longest note token (the default):

\editor.alignment long

These apply at once if alignment is on, otherwise the next
time it is turned on.
Turning alignment on aligns all the bar definitions of
the pane, and while it is on, every edit of a melodic
line (typing or deleting a character, pasting, or
deleting in normal mode, e.g. with x or dw) aligns its
bar again, with the cursor staying where it is in the
token you edit. A space typed after a token moves the
cursor to where the next note starts. While a token is
half typed and can't be read (e.g. c14 on the way to c16,
or a first note left without its duration), the bar is
left as it is until it can. Lines that can't be aligned
from their text (copied bars, \euclide, \transpose,
\apply, repetitions, or lines with comments) are left as
they are. To go back to single spaces between the tokens,
call:

\editor.alignment off

To turn off the automatic closing of brackets and quotes,
where typing an opening bracket also inserts the closing
one, call:

\editor.autobrackets off

and to turn it back on (the default):

\editor.autobrackets on

To turn off the pop-up of autocompletion, which suggests
commands, methods, and names while typing, call:

\editor.autocomplete off

and to turn it back on (the default):

\editor.autocomplete on

Press Tab to select a suggestion and Return to insert it.
Turning it off also clears any suggestions shown.
In Python panes, the suggestions include Python's keywords
and builtins, the names written in the pane, and the names
that executed code has imported or defined (e.g. after
executing import numpy as np, or the pyo objects, which
are always imported). Right after a dot, as in a., they
are the attributes and methods of the object before the
dot, once its code has been executed.
Typing an opening round bracket after the name of a class
or a function, as in Sine(, shows its arguments, dimmed,
between the brackets:

Sine(freq=1000, phase=0, mul=1, add=0)

What you type replaces them, argument by argument, so
typing 440, leaves phase=0, mul=1, add=0, and typing mu
shows the rest of mul=1 and the arguments that are left.
Press Tab to insert what is left of them. While you type
the start of an argument's name, the pop-up is not shown,
so that Tab completes the argument. \editor.autocomplete
off turns these off too.

The settings concern only the pane where the command is
executed, so each pane can be set separately.
Any other method results in an "unknown editor command"
error.
